    "product_id": "98f88614-b50b-4d96-8865-39b33dfa63b8",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 080906\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n206 AM MST Thu Oct 8 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry conditions along with temperatures running about 10 degrees \n  above normal will persist through Friday.\n\n- An Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect for portions of\n  southeast California and southwest Arizona through Friday as\n  temperatures warm to near record levels, resulting in areas of \n  major HeatRisk.\n\n- A much more unsettled weather pattern is expected this weekend\n  through early next week as a series of weather systems affect\n  the region resulting in much cooler temperatures, increasing\n  winds, and elevated rain chances. \n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nLatest objective analysis shows a broad ridge of high pressure in \nplace across the region while a very weak shortwave is situated near \nthe Four Corners. This shortwave in combination with enough mid-\nlevel moisture will likely produce another round of isolated to \nwidely scattered showers across the White Mountains once again \nfor this afternoon. Otherwise, with high pressure continuing to \nremain the dominant feature over much of the region, dry \nconditions will persist through Friday. \n\nWith 500 mb heights ranging between 586-589 dam, afternoon high \ntemperatures will once again be running about 10 degrees above \nnormal across the region as lower desert highs will range between \n100-103 degrees across south-central AZ and 102-109 degrees across \nthe western deserts. With temperatures nearing daily records at both \nYuma and El Centro, areas of Major HeatRisk will continue and thus \nExtreme Heat Warnings are in effect for the Imperial, Lower Colorado \nRiver, and Gila River Valleys as well as for Yuma. Even though \nthe ridge will be on a weakening phase heading into Friday, low-\nlevel thermal profiles will not change that much and thus another\nday of widespread triple digits will continue, although likely a \ndegree or two cooler from today. Areas of Major HeatRisk will \ncontinue to be prominent across the Imperial Valley and Yuma area\nand thus Extreme Heat Warnings remain in effect for these areas. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nA major pattern shift is still on schedule for the weekend \nthrough early next week as a troughing pattern will set up across \nmuch of the western third of the CONUS. A large northern stream \ntrough is expected to dive into the Pacific Northwest on Saturday \nbefore diving into CA by Sunday. In the meantime, a shortwave \ntrough sweeping across the eastern Pacific will pick up Tropical\nStorm Rachel with both systems to be steered northeastward out \nahead of the large west coast trough. The latest track forecast \nfrom the National Hurricane Center continues to show Rachel \ntracking into the far northern part of the Baja Peninsula late \nSaturday night before accelerating north-northeastward through \nthe Colorado River Valley during the day Sunday as a post- \ntropical storm. South to southwest flow aloft out ahead of the \nPacific trough and Tropical Storm Rachel will advect significant \nmoisture into the Desert Southwest during the day on Saturday with\nboth the EPS and GEFS showing PWATs exceeding 200-250% of normal \nby Saturday evening. The increasing moisture combined with an \nupper- level jet streak moving through the Great Basin, increasing\nthe upper-level forcing for ascent, will cause areas of scattered\nshowers and isolated thunderstorms to break out, mainly over \ncentral and southern AZ Saturday afternoon/evening. \n\nAs both the Pacific shortwave and the remnants of Rachel move \nthrough late Saturday night through Sunday, even deeper moisture \nwill be pulled into the region and this will likely be the \ntimeframe for the best rain chances across the region with NBM \nPoPs at greater than 50-60% area wide. There continues to be \nquite a bit uncertainty with the rainfall amounts as it will be \ndependent on the eventual track of the remnants of Rachel and will\nlikely have to wait for the hi-res guidance data to have better \nconfidence on amounts. In addition to the rainfall activity, winds\nwill likely be another hazard to deal with, especially for areas \neast of the track of Rachel as the guidance is depicting 850 mb \nwinds to exceed 40-50 kts, which will likely translate to surface \nwind gusts in excess of 40 mph. As both the Pacific shortwave and \nthe remnants of Rachel lift to the north late Sunday through \nMonday, subsidence left behind will result in decreasing rain \nchances during this period before chances increase once again, \nmainly for south-central and eastern AZ, on Tuesday as the larger \nwest coast trough progresses eastward.\n\nWith the troughing pattern expected to take place this weekend \nthrough early next week along with the increasing cloud cover and \nmoisture, temperatures are expected to take a nose dive. Afternoon \nhighs across the lower deserts will go from the low to mid 90s on \nSaturday to low 80s on Monday, with many areas Tuesday and Wednesday \nlikely topping out in the 70s.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0500Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo weather concerns will exist through Thursday night under \noccasional midlevel cloud decks. Confidence is very good that light \neast winds will prevail through mid/late afternoon before attempting \nto become W/SW. However, this switch may not completed across the \nentire Phoenix metro, and a variable component may occur for several \nhours before reverting back to easterly early Thursday evening. \n\n \nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo weather concerns will exist through Thursday night under clear \nskies. Extended periods of light and variable winds will persist \nwith a general trend for directions to vary between northerly and \nwesterly.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions along well-above normal temperatures will continue \nthrough Friday as lower desert highs reach into the triple \ndigits. Winds today generally be light and diurnally driven \nbefore increasing Friday and into the weekend. Afternoon MinRHs \nthrough Friday will range between 15-25% with fair to good \novernight recoveries (35-60%). A much more unsettled weather \npattern is expected this weekend through early next week as a \nseries of weather systems affect the area with much cooler \ntemperatures along with increasing moisture and chances for \nwetting rainfall. \n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...\n\nRecord highs:\n \nDate Yuma      El Centro\n----     ----     ---------\nOct 8 107 in 1996    106 in 1996\nOct 9 106 in 2024104 in 1996\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ532.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM this morning to 8 PM MST this \n     evening for AZZ530-536.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Friday for CAZ566-567.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM this morning to 8 PM PDT this \n     evening for CAZ569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Lojero \nAVIATION...18 \nFIRE WEATHER...Lojero \nCLIMATE...RW\n",
